<!-- ============================================================
     BlueHammer — CVE-2026-33825
     Windows Defender LPE → SYSTEM via Volume Shadow Copy TOCTOU
     MITRE ATT&CK:
       T1068   — Exploitation for Privilege Escalation
       T1562.001 — Impair Defenses: Disable or Modify Tools
       T1543.003 — Create or Modify System Process: Windows Service
       T1003.001 — OS Credential Dumping: LSASS Memory (post-LPE stage)
       T1055   — Process Injection (post-LPE stage)
     Rule IDs: 103864-103872
     CISA KEV: Active exploitation confirmed by CISA (Jun/Jul 2026)
     Author: SecOps Engineer (Purple Team)
     Date: 2026-07-13
     References:
       - https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-33825
       - https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/cisa-windows-bluehammer-flaw-now-exploited-by-ransomware-gangs/
       - https://www.picussecurity.com/resource/blog/bluehammer-redsun-windows-defender-cve-2026-33825-zero-day-vulnerability-explained
       - https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1068/

     Vulnerability summary:
       CVE-2026-33825 "BlueHammer" is a TOCTOU race condition in Microsoft
       Defender's threat remediation engine (MsMpEng.exe). An attacker with
       low-privilege code execution escalates to SYSTEM by:

         1. Staging a file that triggers a Defender detection
         2. Using an opportunistic lock (oplock) to pause MsMpEng.exe
            at the point where it opens the flagged file
         3. During the pause, creating an NTFS junction from the attacker-
            controlled temporary path to C:\Windows\System32
         4. When MsMpEng.exe resumes and writes to the "remediated" path,
            it writes attacker-controlled content into System32 as SYSTEM

       Real-world ransomware campaigns (late June–July 2026) pair BlueHammer
       with:
         - UnDefend: Defender real-time protection disabler (T1562.001)
         - BeigeBurrow: Go-based C2 tunneling agent
         - Credential dumping via samlib.dll load into non-LSASS processes

       Staged from user-writable paths (Pictures, short Downloads subfolders)
       to reduce EDR scrutiny compared to %TEMP%/%AppData%.

     Log source prerequisites:
       - Wazuh agent with Sysmon installed (EventChannel monitoring)
       - Sysmon config capturing:
           Event 1  — ProcessCreate
           Event 7  — ImageLoaded (DLL loads)
           Event 11 — FileCreate
           Event 12 — RegistryEvent (CreateKey)
           Event 13 — RegistryEvent (SetValue)
           Event 4697 Windows Security log — A service was installed in the system

     Severity rationale:
       0   — Base: MsMpEng.exe file create event (no alert alone)
       0   — Base: samlib.dll image load event (no alert alone)
       13  — MsMpEng.exe FileCreate in System32/driver paths (TOCTOU write)
       13  — samlib.dll loaded by non-LSASS process (credential dump setup)
       12  — GUID-named service created (BlueHammer staging service pattern)
       13  — LSA boot key registry read by non-SYSTEM process
       14  — MsMpEng System32 write + GUID service within 5 min (exploitation chain)
       13  — Defender real-time protection disabled (UnDefend/T1562.001)
       15  — MsMpEng System32 write + Defender disable within 10 min (confirmed LPE chain)
     ============================================================ -->

<group name="privilege_escalation,defense_evasion,bluehammer,cve_2026_33825,">

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 103864: Base anchor — MsMpEng.exe FileCreate in System32
       Windows Defender's remediation engine (MsMpEng.exe) writing
       files to System32 or the Windows driver store is the core
       behavior of BlueHammer TOCTOU exploitation. Under normal
       conditions MsMpEng.exe quarantines/remediates files in the
       Defender quarantine folder, not in System32 directly.
       Level 0: base anchor — no alert alone; chains to 103866.
       Log source: Sysmon Event 11 (FileCreate) via Wazuh EventChannel
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="103864" level="0">
    <if_sid>61612</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\MsMpEng\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetFilename" type="pcre2">(?i)\\(Windows\\System32|Windows\\SysWOW64|Windows\\System|ProgramData\\Microsoft\\Windows Defender|Windows\\inf|Windows\\drivers|Windows\\WinSxS)\\</field>
    <description>Defender MsMpEng.exe creating file in protected system path: $(win.eventdata.targetFilename) (BlueHammer CVE-2026-33825 base anchor)</description>
    <group>bluehammer,defender,file_create,cve_2026_33825,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 103865: Base anchor — samlib.dll loaded by any process
       samlib.dll (SAM local account credential library) is loaded by
       LSASS.exe for credential operations. BlueHammer post-exploitation
       chains include loading samlib.dll into non-LSASS processes
       to perform credential dumping (T1003.001). Level 0: no alert
       alone; chains to 103867.
       Log source: Sysmon Event 7 (ImageLoaded) via Wazuh EventChannel
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="103865" level="0">
    <if_sid>61607</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.imageLoaded" type="pcre2">(?i)\\samlib\.dll$</field>
    <description>samlib.dll loaded by $(win.eventdata.image) (BlueHammer post-exploitation base anchor)</description>
    <group>bluehammer,credential_access,dll_load,cve_2026_33825,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 103866: MsMpEng.exe writes to System32 — TOCTOU write
       When BlueHammer's junction redirect is in place and MsMpEng
       resumes from the oplock, it writes attacker content to
       C:\Windows\System32 instead of the temporary remediation path.
       This Sysmon Event 11 in System32 from MsMpEng.exe is the
       core exploit-success indicator (previously anchor 103864).
       Level 13: high-confidence indicator of active BlueHammer.
       Log source: Sysmon Event 11 (FileCreate) via Wazuh EventChannel
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="103866" level="13">
    <if_sid>103864</if_sid>
    <description>BlueHammer CVE-2026-33825: MsMpEng.exe wrote file to protected system path $(win.eventdata.targetFilename) - TOCTOU NTFS junction likely in place; Defender remediation redirected to System32 (T1068); verify file and initiate containment</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1068</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>bluehammer,defender,file_create,toctou,cve_2026_33825,privilege_escalation,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 103867: samlib.dll loaded by non-LSASS process
       Loading samlib.dll outside of lsass.exe is a known credential
       dumping technique (T1003.001). In BlueHammer post-exploitation
       campaigns (paired with BeigeBurrow C2), attackers load samlib
       into MsMpEng.exe or injected processes after achieving SYSTEM
       to harvest local account credentials without Mimikatz (lower
       EDR signature). lsass.exe loads are expected; anything else
       warrants investigation.
       Level 13: high-confidence credential theft indicator.
       Log source: Sysmon Event 7 (ImageLoaded) via Wazuh EventChannel
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="103867" level="13">
    <if_sid>103865</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2" negate="yes">(?i)\\lsass\.exe$</field>
    <description>BlueHammer CVE-2026-33825: samlib.dll loaded by $(win.eventdata.image) (not LSASS) - credential dumping via SAM library; post-BlueHammer SYSTEM-level credential theft likely (T1003.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1003.001</id>
      <id>T1068</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>bluehammer,credential_access,dll_load,samlib,cve_2026_33825,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 103868: GUID-named service created
       BlueHammer and BeigeBurrow ransomware campaigns create temporary
       services with GUID-format names (e.g. {A8F2E3C1-4D7B-...}) to
       execute SYSTEM-level payloads and establish persistence.
       Windows Event 4697 (A service was installed in the system)
       with a GUID-pattern name is highly indicative of this technique.
       Level 12: unusual; most legitimate services have readable names.
       Log source: Windows Security Event 4697 via Wazuh
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="103868" level="12">
    <if_sid>60013</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.serviceName" type="pcre2">(?i)\{[0-9A-Fa-f]{8}-[0-9A-Fa-f]{4}-[0-9A-Fa-f]{4}-[0-9A-Fa-f]{4}-[0-9A-Fa-f]{12}\}</field>
    <description>BlueHammer CVE-2026-33825: GUID-named service installed: $(win.eventdata.serviceName) by $(win.eventdata.subjectUserName) - BlueHammer/BeigeBurrow post-exploitation persistence pattern (T1543.003)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1543.003</id>
      <id>T1068</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>bluehammer,persistence,service_install,guid_service,cve_2026_33825,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 103869: LSA boot key registry read by non-SYSTEM process
       BlueHammer post-exploitation includes LSA boot key extraction
       (HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\Lsa) to decrypt the SAM
       database. The BlueHammerFix detection rule set specifically calls
       out LSA boot key registry access as an indicator.
       Non-SYSTEM process reading LSA registry keys is suspicious.
       Level 13.
       Log source: Sysmon Event 13 (RegistryEvent SetValue) or
                   Event 12 (CreateKey) via Wazuh EventChannel
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="103869" level="13">
    <if_sid>61614</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.targetObject" type="pcre2">(?i)HKLM\\SYSTEM\\(CurrentControlSet|ControlSet\d+)\\Control\\Lsa\b</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2" negate="yes">(?i)\\(lsass\.exe|services\.exe|svchost\.exe|system)$</field>
    <description>BlueHammer CVE-2026-33825: LSA boot key registry path accessed by $(win.eventdata.image) (not LSASS/services/svchost) - SAM database decryption setup; post-BlueHammer credential harvest in progress (T1003.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1003.001</id>
      <id>T1552.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>bluehammer,credential_access,registry,lsa_access,cve_2026_33825,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 103870: MsMpEng System32 write + GUID service (exploit chain)
       The two-indicator chain of:
         1. MsMpEng.exe writing to System32 (rule 103864/103866)
         2. A GUID-named service being installed (rule 103868)
       within 5 minutes from the same host is the BlueHammer
       escalation-to-persistence chain observed in BeigeBurrow campaigns.
       The GUID service is created after SYSTEM is achieved to load the
       tunneling C2 agent. Level 14: near-certain exploitation.
       Log source: Sysmon Event 11 + Windows Security Event 4697
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="103870" level="14" timeframe="300">
    <if_matched_sid>103864</if_matched_sid>
    <if_sid>103868</if_sid>
    <description>BlueHammer CVE-2026-33825 EXPLOIT CHAIN: MsMpEng System32 write THEN GUID service install within 5 min - high-confidence BlueHammer LPE succeeded; BeigeBurrow C2 likely being staged (T1068/T1543.003); isolate host immediately</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1068</id>
      <id>T1543.003</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>bluehammer,exploitation_chain,privilege_escalation,persistence,cve_2026_33825,critical,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 103871: Defender real-time protection disabled (UnDefend)
       BlueHammer ransomware campaigns deploy UnDefend to disable
       Defender's real-time protection immediately after achieving SYSTEM.
       This suppresses further detection before credential dumping and
       ransomware deployment. The Windows Security Event for disabling
       AV real-time protection is a post-LPE indicator (T1562.001).
       Level 13.
       Log source: Windows Security Event 5001 (Defender disabled)
                   or Sysmon Event 13 (Defender registry key write)
                   via Wazuh
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="103871" level="13">
    <if_sid>61710</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.name" type="pcre2">(?i)(Windows Defender|Microsoft Defender|Real-time protection)</field>
    <description>BlueHammer CVE-2026-33825: Windows Defender real-time protection disabled - UnDefend tool pattern; post-BlueHammer LPE step before credential dump and ransomware deployment (T1562.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1562.001</id>
      <id>T1068</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>bluehammer,defense_evasion,defender_disabled,cve_2026_33825,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Rule 103872: MsMpEng System32 write + Defender disable (confirmed LPE)
       The highest-confidence BlueHammer indicator: Defender's own
       engine (MsMpEng) writing to System32 AND Defender being disabled
       within 10 minutes. This is the exact BlueHammer → UnDefend chain
       documented in active ransomware campaigns (Jun–Jul 2026).
       After SYSTEM is achieved, UnDefend disables Defender before
       BeigeBurrow C2 and ransomware are deployed.
       Level 15: confirmed exploitation; initiate immediate response.
       Log source: Sysmon Event 11 + Windows Security Event 5001
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="103872" level="15" timeframe="600">
    <if_matched_sid>103864</if_matched_sid>
    <if_sid>103871</if_sid>
    <description>BlueHammer CVE-2026-33825 CONFIRMED LPE: MsMpEng System32 write then Defender disabled within 10 min - BlueHammer LPE + UnDefend chain confirmed; BeigeBurrow C2 and ransomware deployment imminent (T1068/T1562.001); ISOLATE HOST IMMEDIATELY</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1068</id>
      <id>T1562.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>bluehammer,exploitation_chain,privilege_escalation,defense_evasion,ransomware,cve_2026_33825,critical,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
